# ============================================================================
# Cocorra — deployment secrets template
# ============================================================================
#
# Copy this file to `.env` next to docker-compose.yml and fill in real values.
# `.env` is gitignored. THIS file is tracked, so it must never contain a real
# value — only placeholders.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   # edit .env
#   docker compose up -d
#
# docker-compose reads `.env` automatically. Any variable referenced in
# docker-compose.yml with the `${VAR:?message}` form will abort `up` with that
# message if it is missing, so a forgotten secret fails at deploy time rather
# than becoming a silent insecure default.
# ============================================================================


# ── LOCAL DEVELOPMENT DOES NOT USE THIS FILE ────────────────────────────────
#
# This file is for PRODUCTION, read by docker-compose. For `dotnet run` or
# `dotnet watch run` on your own machine, use user-secrets instead — it stores
# the value outside the repository, so it cannot be committed by accident:
#
#     dotnet user-secrets set "Analytics:IpHashSalt" "$(openssl rand -base64 32)" --project Cocorra.API
#
# Or, without openssl:
#
#     dotnet user-secrets set "Analytics:IpHashSalt" "any-long-random-dev-only-string" --project Cocorra.API
#
# It is loaded automatically because ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT is Development in
# launchSettings.json. Note the consequence: user secrets load ONLY in
# Development. Running locally with --no-launch-profile, or with
# ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production, will still fail the startup guard — which
# is correct, because a production environment must take the salt from the
# environment, not from a developer's machine.
#
# Two mechanisms, two environments, one rule: the salt is never in git.


# ── REQUIRED ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Salt for pseudonymising IP addresses in the analytics event store.
#
# Generate a fresh 32-byte value and keep it OUT of source control:
#
#     openssl rand -base64 32
#
# WHY IT MUST BE SECRET: UserEvent.IpHash stores SHA256(salt + ip). The IPv4
# space is 2^32 addresses, so anyone who knows the salt can hash every possible
# address and reverse the pseudonymisation completely. The salt is the only
# thing standing between a hashed column and a plaintext IP log.
#
# WHY IT MUST NOT CHANGE CASUALLY: rotating it means hashes written before and
# after the rotation are uncorrelatable. Nothing in the current codebase reads
# IpHash, so rotation has no effect on any served metric today — but it does
# permanently foreclose any future analysis that spans the rotation boundary.
# See docs/dashboard-discovery/PRODUCTION-READINESS-REPORT.md section 2.
#
ANALYTICS_IP_HASH_SALT=CHANGE_ME_run_openssl_rand_base64_32


# ── OPTIONAL — analytics staged activation ──────────────────────────────────
#
# Leave these unset for the initial deployment. Both event-emission flags
# default to false in appsettings.json, which means Stage A runs with the
# pipeline live and no new events emitting — exactly what Stage A is for.
#
# Enable them ONE AT A TIME, only after the corresponding gate has passed in
# docs/dashboard-discovery/30-production-deployment-checklist.md.
# The flags are conjunctive in code: EnableHighFrequencyEvents does nothing
# unless EnableNewEventEmission is also true.

# Stage B — low-frequency core-loop events
# Analytics__EnableNewEventEmission=true

# Stage C — high-frequency events. Raise the channel capacity in a SEPARATE,
# EARLIER deploy so a drop-rate spike can be attributed to one change or the other.
# Analytics__EventChannelCapacity=25000
# Analytics__EnableHighFrequencyEvents=true

# Optional durable log sink. Must point at a mounted volume path, or it is lost
# on container restart. Unset keeps the current stdout-only behaviour.
# Analytics__StructuredLogPath=/app/logs/cocorra-structured.log


# ── TRANSACTIONAL EMAIL (Resend) ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# EmailService sends OTP, password-reset and admin notification mail through
# the Resend HTTP API. The key is read at send time, so the app starts without
# it — but every email send fails (and throws) until it is set.
#
# The service checks TWO configuration keys in priority order (both read through
# IConfiguration, which already includes environment variables):
#   1. EmailSettings:ResendApiKey  (appsettings / user-secrets / EmailSettings__ResendApiKey env var)
#   2. RESEND_API_KEY  (standalone env var — fallback, e.g. for Docker / CI)
#
# Create an API key with "Sending access" at https://resend.com/api-keys.
# For local development use user-secrets instead:
#
#     dotnet user-secrets set "EmailSettings:ResendApiKey" "<key>" --project Cocorra.API
#
# Uncomment ONE of the following when deploying:
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# EmailSettings__ResendApiKey=

# Default sender: Cocorra Support <support@cocorraapp.com>
# Sender address. MUST be an address on a domain verified in Resend, or Resend
# rejects the send. Unset keeps the default support@cocorraapp.com.
# EmailSettings__FromEmail=support@cocorraapp.com
# EmailSettings__FromName=Cocorra Support


# ── REVERSE PROXY / FORWARDED HEADERS ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# The API honours X-Forwarded-For / X-Forwarded-Proto ONLY from trusted proxies,
# so per-IP rate limiting sees the real client address behind nginx/Caddy/a load
# balancer. List each proxy IP (one per index). Left empty, only loopback proxies
# are trusted (the framework default) — never "trust everything".
#
ForwardedHeaders__KnownProxies__0=127.0.0.1
ForwardedHeaders__KnownProxies__1=172.16.4.1
ForwardedHeaders__KnownNetworks__0=172.16.0.0/12


# ── PRODUCTION SECRETS (EXTERNALIZED) ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# All production secrets are supplied externally via this .env file.
# The tracked appsettings.json contains ONLY safe local development placeholders.
#
# Generate a new random JWT key (min 32 bytes):
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Database connection string:
# ConnectionStrings__DefaultConnection=Server=cocorra_db,1433;Database=CocorraDb;User Id=sa;Password=<YOUR_ROTATED_DB_PASSWORD>;TrustServerCertificate=True;MultipleActiveResultSets=True;
#
# JWT Signing Key (Must be at least 256-bit / 32-character random string):
# JWTSetting__securityKey=
#
# Initial Administrator Account:
# SeedAdmin__Email=admin@cocorraapp.com
# SeedAdmin__Password=
#
# LiveKit SFU & TURN credentials:
# LiveKit__ApiKey=
# LiveKit__ApiSecret=
# LiveKit__IceServers__1__credential=
#
# MinIO Object Storage:
# Minio__AccessKey=
# Minio__SecretKey=
